wer station at 106 Bourke Street, Carrington, New South Wales, Australia. It was built from 1877 to 1878 by William H. Jennings. It is also known as Hydraulic Engine House and Crane Bases Nos.","wpu":"Carrington Pump House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ce/Carrington_Power.JPG/960px-Carrington_Power.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Frederick Ash Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9272,"lo":151.773,"d":822,"ds":"Frederick Ash Building is a heritage-listed warehouse and retail premises at 359–361 Hunter Street, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. It was designed by Frederick B. Menkens and built from 1904 to 1905. It was added to the New South Wales State Heritage Register on 2 April 1999.","wpu":"Frederick Ash Building","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c1/642_-_Frederick_Ash_Building_%285045387b1%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"David Cohen & Co. Warehouse","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9288,"lo":151.7824,"d":1197,"ds":"David Cohen & Co. Warehouse is a heritage-listed facade and side wall from a former warehouse at 48–50 Bolton Street in the Newcastle Central Business District, New South Wales, Australia. It was designed by Frederick B. Menckens and built from 1901.","wpu":"David Cohen & Co. Warehouse","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/David_Cohen_%26_Co._Warehouse_-_Facade_%26_Side_Wall.jpg/960px-David_Cohen_%26_Co._Warehouse_-_Facade_%26_Side_Wall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Christ Church Cathedral, Newcastle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.929722,"lo":151.780556,"d":1198,"ds":"The Cathedral Church of Christ the King, also called Christ Church Cathedral, is an Anglican cathedral in Newcastle, New South Wales. It is the cathedral church of the Diocese of Newcastle in the Anglican Church of Australia. The building, designed by John Horbury Hunt in the Gothic Revival style, is located on a hill at the city's eastern end in the suburb called The Hill.","wpu":"Christ Church Cathedral, Newcastle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/2_christ_church_cathedral.jpg/960px-2_christ_church_cathedral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Manufacturers House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9287,"lo":151.7842,"d":1293,"ds":"Manufacturers House is a heritage-listed office building at 35–37 Watt Street, Newcastle, City of Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. It was designed by Spain and Cosh and built in the 1920s. It is also known as Manufacturer's Mutual Building.","wpu":"Manufacturers House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/05/Manufacturers_House.jpg/960px-Manufacturers_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Earp Gillam Bond Store","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9267,"lo":151.7868,"d":1329,"ds":"The Earp Gillam Bond Store is a heritage-listed former bond store at 16 Telford Street, Newcastle East, in the Hunter region of New South Wales, Australia. It was added to the New South Wales State Heritage Register on 2 April 1999.","wpu":"Earp Gillam Bond Store","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Earp_Gillam_Bond_Store.jpg/960px-Earp_Gillam_Bond_Store.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Coal River Precinct","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9242,"lo":151.7921,"d":1663,"ds":"Coal River Precinct is a heritage-listed historic precinct at Nobbys Road, Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. It was built from 1804 to 1960. It includes Fort Scratchley, Nobbys Head, the Convict Lumber Yard site, Macquarie Pier, the breakwater and Nobby's Beach.","wpu":"Coal River Precinct","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Nobbys_Head.jpg/960px-Nobbys_Head.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St John's Anglican Church, Newcastle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9341,"lo":151.768453,"d":1682,"ds":"The St John's Anglican Church, formally the Church of St John the Evangelist, also called St John's Cooks Hill, is an Anglican church in Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. It is the oldest remaining church building in Newcastle, completed in 1860.","wpu":"St John's Anglican Church, Newcastle","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/58/SLNSW_41512_St_Johns_Church_of_England_Cooks_Hill.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Bogey Hole","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.9351,"lo":151.7817,"d":1791,"ds":"The Bogey Hole, also known as the Commandant's Baths, is a heritage-listed sea bath in Newcastle in New South Wales, Australia. It is thought to be the oldest surviving European construction in the city area. The pool was hewn from a sandstone/conglomerate rock shelf at the base of cliffs near Shepherds Hill.","wpu":"Bogey Hole","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Bogey_Hole_in_Newcastle.jpg/960px-Bogey_Hole_in_Newcastle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sacred Heart Cathedral, Hamilton","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":-32.923109,"lo":151.753782,"d":2010,"ds":"Sacred Heart Cathedral, Hamilton, is the cathedral church of the Roman Catholic Diocese of Maitland-Newcastle and the seat and residence of the Catholic Bishop of Maitland-Newcastle, New South Wales, Australia. The position is currently held by Bishop Michael Kennedy, who was installed on 2 February 2023 following the death of Bishop William Wright on 13 November 2021.","wpu":"Sacred Heart Cathedral, Hamilton","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/44/NewcastleNSW_SacredHeartCathedral_b.jpg/960px-NewcastleNSW_SacredHeartCathedral_b.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Newcastle, also known as Greater Newcastle (locally NEW-kah-səl; Awabakal: Mulubinba), is a large metropolitan area and the second-most-populous such area of New South Wales, Australia. It includes the cities of Newcastle and Lake Macquarie and it is the hub of the Lower Hunter region, which includes most parts of the cities of Newcastle, Lake Macquarie, Maitland, Cessnock, and Port Stephens Council. Newcastle is also known by its colloquial nickname, Newy.","wpu":"Newcastle, New South Wales","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/The_Custom_House%2C_Newcastle.jpg/960px-The_Custom_House%2C_Newcastle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}